[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Buffer Overflow and Binary Exploitation Basics"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Binary Exploitation"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Binary exploitation is a fundamental concept in ethical hacking and cybersecurity. It involves leveraging vulnerabilities in compiled applications, such as C or C++ programs, to manipulate the execution flow. The most classic and prevalent of these vulnerabilities is the stack-based buffer overflow."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Memory Layout"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"To understand buffer overflows, one must understand how a program organizes its memory when running. The process memory is typically divided into several segments: the Text segment (executable code), Data segment (initialized global variables), BSS (uninitialized global variables), the Heap (dynamically allocated memory), and the Stack."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The Stack grows downward from high memory addresses to low memory addresses."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"It stores local variables, function parameters, and return addresses."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The Instruction Pointer (EIP/RIP) tracks the current execution location."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Mechanics of a Buffer Overflow"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"A buffer overflow occurs when a program attempts to write more data to a fixed-length block of memory (a buffer) than it can hold. Because C lacks built-in bounds checking for functions like strcpy or gets, excess data spills over into adjacent memory regions, potentially overwriting critical data structures like the saved frame pointer and the return address."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

void vulnerable_function(char *input) {
    char buffer[64];
    strcpy(buffer, input); // Vulnerable to buffer overflow
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc > 1) {
        vulnerable_function(argv[1]);
    }
    return 0;
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Exploitation Strategy"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When exploiting a stack overflow, the primary goal is often to redirect the execution flow to malicious shellcode. By supplying a carefully crafted payload consisting of padding, a new return address, and the shellcode itself, the attacker can force the application to execute arbitrary commands."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Determine the exact offset where the return address is overwritten."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Craft a NOP sled (No-Operation instructions) to reliably slide into the shellcode."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 3: Replace the original return address with the memory address pointing to the NOP sled."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigation and Defense"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern operating systems implement several exploit mitigations to prevent buffer overflows from being easily weaponized. Security professionals and developers must understand these defenses to secure applications properly."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization): Randomizes the memory locations of program segments."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DEP/NX (Data Execution Prevention / Non-Executable Stack): Marks memory regions as non-executable to prevent shellcode execution on the stack."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Stack Canaries: Places a known value before the return address and checks it before returning to detect tampering."}]
